{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba WORDING-V2.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# A sharpened link question, version 2 (D703; for council review before its cents probe)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex chose to probe a sharpened wording before the paid run (D703): keep Data Center reads total revenue and the 38 negatives, remove\n     4\tunsupported links including the base effect, no blanket ban on a whole reading its part. The probe is harness-only: `window_test.py\n     5\tprobe-v2` replaces the READS and SIGN criteria texts in memory for its pass; no product file changes until a version is chosen.\n     6\t\n     7\t## 1. Prompt audit (the census's failure classes against the request sentences; AGENTS §6)\n     8\t\n     9\t| Failure class (census, `CENSUS-RESULT.md`) | The request sentence that produced it | The standard or decision on the case | Verdict |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| Part against whole, direction unsettled: v9–v14 at margins 0.03–0.09; v21–v9 flips between draws; whole-to-part and part-to-whole both occur | READS `candidate_reads_thesis`: \"a part read against its whole (...), or a quantity read against another it depends on (...)\": the second clause lets a whole \"depend on\" its part; no sentence says which reads when both fit | D537: the total carried the year's consensus and guidance that the segment's requests lacked; D260: a judgement is made against what the market expects (consensus, guidance, the stated plan) | **abstract** (two clauses, both directions, no tie-break) |\n    12\t| Across periods with an unsupported sign: v17 (FY2028 growth) reads v16 (FY2027 CPU), together, 3 of 3; v35 (FY2028 total) reads v9 (FY2027 Data Center), 1 of 3 | no READS sentence on periods (`neither` covers only \"the same quantity for the same period and scope\"); every SIGN example is within one period | the arithmetic of a growth rate (a higher base lowers next year's growth at a given level); the dependency design §2: another period of the same quantity \"may be linked\" | **missing** |\n    13\t\n    14\t## 2. The wording (changes marked; every example from another industry, none names the covered company)\n    15\t\n    16\tREADS, `candidate_reads_thesis`, appended sentence:\n    17\t> When either question could be judged with the other in view, the one whose answer the supplied text anchors less (no guidance,\n    18\t> consensus or reported figure for its period) is the one judged with the other's conclusion in view.\n    19\t\n    20\tREADS, `neither`, appended clause (before \"sharing a company ...\"):\n    21\t> or quantities of different periods when the supplied text does not state how one period's answer moves the other's (a retailer's\n    22\t> sales this year and its sales growth next year)\n    23\t\n    24\tSIGN, `opposite`, added example:\n    25\t> ...; one year's sales and the next year's sales growth at a given next-year level, since a higher base lowers the growth\n    26\t\n    27\t`thesis_reads_candidate` (\"for the same reasons with the roles swapped\") and the instructions are unchanged, so the tie-break applies\n    28\tboth ways: a part with guidance and a whole without would have the whole read the part. No direction is banned.\n    29\t\n    30\tSources: D537 and D260 for the tie-break; the growth-rate definition for the base effect; the dependency design §2 (\"another period of\n    31\tthe same quantity may be linked\") narrowed to \"when the supplied text states how\": a link across periods still exists when the text\n    32\tgives the mechanism. Growth: 522 characters across the three criteria texts (+227 READS candidate_reads_thesis, +178 READS neither, +117 SIGN opposite) (the READS criteria repeat in every pair of a job,\n    33\tabout 110 tokens a pair); written reason: the audit shows a missing case and an abstract clause, and nothing in the current texts\n    34\tduplicates them to cut.\n    35\t\n    36\t## 3. What the probe decides (registered before its answers: `expected-links-v2.json`)\n    37\t\n    38\tThe registration of the census stands (R 1, W 38, O 66), plus one **F** class: the base-effect sign named by council R round 17, a\n    39\tgrowth rate reading an earlier period's revenue amount with the sign together (v17 reads v8, v9, v14 or v16, together: 4 pairs). Gate\n    40\tfor version 2: the known positive right in 3 of 3 draws, no W link, no F link. Reported beside: every O pair against the current\n    41\twording's census, and the margins.\n    42\t- **Pass:** version 2 becomes the candidate for the paid design (a product version built and reviewed first).\n    43\t- **Fail on the known positive or a W link:** the current wording stays the candidate with its measured risks (D702).\n    44\t- **Fail on F only:** the base-effect sentence did not take; reported, the current wording's risk stands.\n    45\t\n    46\t## 4. The probe\n    47\t\n    48\t`window_test.py probe-v2 <scratch> <draw> <ledger> 0.10`: the census pass with the version-2 texts patched into `links.READS` and\n    49\t`links.SIGN` for the pass; every link job is new (the July answers do not match the new texts), so the seq-148 jobs are bought. Three\n    50\tdraws on D700's ledger (spent $0.0266, $0.0734 left); priced as the census (a draw settles about $0.009 at the ledger's rule; the READS\n    51\trequests grow by about 110 tokens a pair). The completeness check, equality with old-2 and the paid-transport record apply as in the\n    52\tcensus; `draw.json` records the wording.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/links.py | sed -n '1,125p'; nl -ba ../../../docs/prd/06-views.md | sed -n '110,145p'; rg -n 'D537|D703|D700' ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -15\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/links.py | sed -n '1,125p'; nl -ba ../../../docs/prd/06-views.md | sed -n '110,145p'; rg -n 'D537|D703|D700' ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -15\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"Links between number questions, chosen in the matching step (D51, D368 item 2, D694, D695; the design and its four tables:\n     2\tdocs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/DESIGN.md).\n     3\t\n     4\tUnder the matching engine `jev-links` only, after matching has settled which candidates open: for each newly opened number question\n     5\tC and each open number question T on C's list, Jev is asked whether one should be judged with the other's conclusion in view (READS)\n     6\tand, for a pair that should, which way the read question moves the reading one (SIGN). Code decides only identity: which pairs are\n     7\tnumber questions, the canonical candidate, circles, and the label the judge is shown (from the sign and the typed rung bounds). A\n     8\tlink is written with kind `reads`: judging reads it as a dependency; filing does not treat it as a narrower home (D694 (a)).\n     9\t\"\"\"\n    10\timport json\n    11\t\n    12\tfrom .. import jev, model, vocabulary\n    13\tfrom . import identity, jev_matching, store\n    14\t\n    15\tENGINE = 'jev-links'\n    16\tKIND = 'reads'\n    17\tTHRESHOLD = jev_matching.THRESHOLD\n    18\tLABELS = vocabulary.ASSET_LABELS                 # five-rung asset ladders run Strongly bullish .. Strongly bearish, or the reverse\n    19\tCANDIDATE_READS, THESIS_READS, NEITHER = 'candidate_reads_thesis', 'thesis_reads_candidate', 'neither'\n    20\tTOGETHER, OPPOSITE, NO_SIGN = 'together', 'opposite', 'no_clear_sign'\n    21\t\n    22\tREADS = {\n    23\t    CANDIDATE_READS: \"the CANDIDATE should be judged with the THESIS's current conclusion in view, because the THESIS's answer bounds \"\n    24\t                     \"or drives the CANDIDATE's: a part read against its whole (one product line's sales against the company's total \"\n    25\t                     \"sales; one region's loan book against the bank's total loans), or a quantity read against another it depends on \"\n    26\t                     \"(a component supplier's revenue against its largest customer's capital spending; an airline's fuel bill against \"\n    27\t                     \"its fuel volume)\",\n    28\t    THESIS_READS: \"the THESIS should be judged with the CANDIDATE's current conclusion in view, for the same reasons with the roles swapped\",\n    29\t    NEITHER: \"neither answer bounds or drives the other: unrelated quantities (a retailer's gross margin and its store openings in \"\n    30\t             \"another country), or the same quantity for the same period and scope; sharing a company, industry or topic is not enough\",\n    31\t}\n    32\tSIGN = {\n    33\t    TOGETHER: \"a higher answer to the question read makes a higher answer to the question reading it more likely (a company's total \"\n    34\t              \"sales and one product line's sales, when that line's share of the total holds steady; a customer's capital spending \"\n    35\t              \"and its component supplier's revenue)\",\n    36\t    OPPOSITE: \"a higher answer to the question read makes a lower answer to the question reading it more likely (an airline's fuel \"\n    37\t              \"bill and its operating margin; a retailer's markdowns and its gross margin)\",\n    38\t    NO_SIGN: \"the question read only caps the other without moving its most likely answer (a regulator's cap on a bank's fees set far \"\n    39\t             \"above what it charges), or the supplied text does not show which way it moves it\",\n    40\t}\n    41\tREADS_INSTRUCTIONS = (\"Should one of these two questions be judged with the other's current conclusion in view? Use only the supplied \"\n    42\t                      \"text. THESIS:\\n\")\n    43\tSIGN_INSTRUCTIONS = {\n    44\t    CANDIDATE_READS: \"If the THESIS's answer turned out higher, which way would that move the CANDIDATE's most likely answer? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\\n\",\n    45\t    THESIS_READS: \"If the CANDIDATE's answer turned out higher, which way would that move the THESIS's most likely answer? Use only the supplied text. THESIS:\\n\",\n    46\t}\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\tclass Refused(RuntimeError):\n    50\t    \"\"\"The link step cannot finish for this article (a persistent circle, an unbound waiting row, a Jev failure); `record` says why.\"\"\"\n    51\t\n    52\t    def __init__(self, message, record):\n    53\t        super().__init__(message)\n    54\t        self.record = record\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\tdef reads_question(text, excluded=None, note=''):\n    58\t    criteria = {k: v for k, v in READS.items() if k != excluded}\n    59\t    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': criteria, 'instructions': READS_INSTRUCTIONS.replace('THESIS:\\n', (note + ' ' if note else '') + 'THESIS:\\n') + text}\n    60\t\n    61\t\n    62\tdef sign_question(text, direction):\n    63\t    return {'type': 'choice', 'criteria': dict(SIGN), 'instructions': SIGN_INSTRUCTIONS[direction] + text}\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\t# ---------- identity: which ladders are number ladders, and the label ----------\n    67\t\n    68\tdef typed_direction(rungs):\n    69\t    \"\"\"-1 when the values fall from the first rung to the last, +1 when they rise, None otherwise. Each rung is [lo, hi) of its typed\n    70\t    bounds; only the first and last rung may leave one outer end open; an interval with lo >= hi is invalid; adjacent rungs may\n    71\t    touch. Prose is never read.\"\"\"\n    72\t    if len(rungs) < 2:\n    73\t        return None\n    74\t    intervals = []\n    75\t    for index, rung in enumerate(rungs):\n    76\t        lo, hi = rung.get('lo'), rung.get('hi')\n    77\t        outer = index in (0, len(rungs) - 1)\n    78\t        if (lo is None and hi is None) or ((lo is None or hi is None) and not outer):\n    79\t            return None\n    80\t        if lo is not None and hi is not None and float(lo) >= float(hi):\n    81\t            return None\n    82\t        intervals.append((None if lo is None else float(lo), None if hi is None else float(hi)))\n    83\t\n    84\t    def at_or_below(lower, upper):\n    85\t        return lower[1] is not None and upper[0] is not None and lower[1] <= upper[0]\n    86\t\n    87\t    pairs = list(zip(intervals, intervals[1:]))\n    88\t    if all(at_or_below(after, before) for before, after in pairs):\n    89\t        return -1\n    90\t    if all(at_or_below(before, after) for before, after in pairs):\n    91\t        return 1\n    92\t    return None\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\tdef favourable_end(rungs):\n    96\t    \"\"\"'first' or 'last': the end whose stored label is Strongly bullish, on a five-rung ladder carrying the canonical labels in order\n    97\t    or reversed; None otherwise.\"\"\"\n    98\t    labels = [vocabulary.canonical('label', r['label']) for r in rungs]\n    99\t    if labels == LABELS:\n   100\t        return 'first'\n   101\t    if labels == LABELS[::-1]:\n   102\t        return 'last'\n   103\t    return None\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\tdef label_for(reader_rungs, read_rungs, sign):\n   107\t    \"\"\"The existing direction label (`first_is_bullish` / `last_is_bullish`): which end of the read question's ladder is favourable for\n   108\t    the reading asset question, given the sign; None when undecidable.\"\"\"\n   109\t    reader_direction, read_direction, favourable = typed_direction(reader_rungs), typed_direction(read_rungs), favourable_end(reader_rungs)\n   110\t    if None in (reader_direction, read_direction, favourable) or sign not in (TOGETHER, OPPOSITE):\n   111\t        return None\n   112\t    reader_high = 'first' if reader_direction == -1 else 'last'\n   113\t    favourable_is_high = favourable == reader_high\n   114\t    want_read_high = favourable_is_high if sign == TOGETHER else not favourable_is_high\n   115\t    read_high = 'first' if read_direction == -1 else 'last'\n   116\t    end = read_high if want_read_high else ('last' if read_high == 'first' else 'first')\n   117\t    return 'first_is_bullish' if end == 'first' else 'last_is_bullish'\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\tdef is_number_question(kind, rungs, measure):\n   121\t    \"\"\"D695: an asset question with a five-rung number ladder (canonical labels, typed direction) and a measure that is not an event.\"\"\"\n   122\t    return (kind == 'asset' and len(rungs) == 5 and bool(measure) and measure not in identity.EVENT_MEASURES\n   123\t            and typed_direction(rungs) is not None and favourable_end(rungs) is not None)\n   124\t\n   125\t\n   110\t- Tier follows who confirmed it. An unnamed-source report that Commerce is issuing licences is a rumour. An official or company confirmation is factual evidence recorded as a revision of it, both in force (D202). The rumour stays a rumour.\n   111\t- Store evidence once without an intrinsic direction. Each rejudgement writes its push on the evidence–thesis link. One statement can affect multiple theses differently.\n   112\t<!-- /generated: tiers -->\n   113\t\n   114\t## Dependencies: reading the arrows\n   115\t\n   116\t<!-- generated: dependencies -->\n   117\t```\n   118\tB depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)\n   119\t├─ Numeric thesis → Read the specified year's thesis (Example: JPM 2027 net interest income → Fed year-end 2027 rate)\n   120\t│   ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n   121\t│   ├─ A is closed → Read A's closing state and reasons (The frozen outcome is what B needs; keep reading it)\n   122\t│   └─ A does not exist yet → Wait for it (Record the awaited year on the chain containing the shown thesis. When that year's successor is created, bind the dependency and rejudge B once.)\n   123\t└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)\n   124\t    ├─ A is open → Read A's state and reasons\n   125\t    ├─ A is closed with a successor → Read the successor (Record the change. The successor changes B's input, so rejudge B once.)\n   126\t    └─ A is closed without a successor → Read A's closing state and reasons, marked “historical only”\n   127\t```\n   128\t\n   129\t- Theme theses do not judge outcomes as good or bad. Each dependent asset thesis records its direction: rate cuts are bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM.\n   130\t- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.\n   131\t- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushes it; no edge is written for it. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.\n   132\t- Count a statement once. A statement about A is A's evidence, including when two candidates arise from one item. B learns it through A's conclusion. Daily filing gives a statement one home: the narrowest open thesis it bears on, as that thesis's own figure, step or estimate or through the demand, supply, competition or pricing behind its answer, plus the same answer's candidates (D207, D259, D264, D265); the separate coverage-start reading was retired (D370). Dependencies are chosen only when B is created; a later item adding a dependency to an existing thesis is decided (D191 point 2) and to be built (D368).\n   133\t- Rejudge an open thesis when its inputs change: new evidence, replacement, a new link to existing evidence, a dependency declaring a changed conclusion, or (for an asset thesis) a theme thesis declaring one. Additional source references alone do not count. Binding an awaited A or moving to its successor also changes B's inputs. Closed theses do not update. A memo-derived thesis is first judged when evidence first links to it; before that, it and its dependants see “not yet judged”.\n   134\t- The matching call chooses a new thesis's dependencies, stating which conclusions it relies on and each direction (D51). Choose only among searched theses, extraction block-5 theses and candidates opened from the same item; no extra search. Jev, the default matcher, is not asked for them, so new theses do not receive dependencies through the default matcher; building the choice on the Jev path is decided (D368). The historical seed questions are retired (D370). A historical seed's dependency arrow means \"supports\" (D368, by D208): for filing, the supporting (driver) thesis is the narrower one; for judging, the supported (root) thesis reads the supporting theses' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged before their roots. The judging code reads the stored edge the other way today; the fix is to be built and measured (D368). Completed events are evidence, not dependencies: today's oil price influencing OPEC output is evidence for the OPEC-output thesis.\n   135\t- Before adding an edge or following it to a successor, code checks for cycles. Refuse a cycle and ask the model to correct it; persistent cycles follow normal failure handling. Apparently mutually dependent questions indicate poorly defined theses.\n   136\t<!-- /generated: dependencies -->\n   137\t\n   138\t## Succession: the same focus over successive periods\n   139\t\n   140\t<!-- generated: chain -->\n   141\t- Successive periods of the same focus form a chain, such as Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 rate → 2028 rate. The chain records period order. Dependency traversal follows the rules above.\n   142\t- One thesis has at most one successor: FY2026 cannot produce two FY2027 successors.\n   143\t- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.\n   144\t<!-- /generated: chain -->\n   145\t\n6457:## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n6491:- **The council's review:** the filing stop condition of D537 is met. A lost filing is not a demonstrated changed answer. The\n10391:  The census of that stretch (D537) found the useful pair to be Data Center revenue reading total revenue.\n10515:## D700 · 2026-11-21 · Alex approves a $0.10 ceiling for the census of the link decisions before the whole-window run\n10525:## D701 · 2026-11-21 · Council R passes the census harness (rounds 13–16); the census of link decisions is sent under D700\n10538:  $0.10 (D700).\n10543:- **Result** (`docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/CENSUS-RESULT.md`, `census-pairs.md`; $0.0266 settled of Alex's $0.10, D700):\n10556:## D703 · 2026-11-21 · Alex chooses to probe a sharpened link question before the paid run\n10565:  within the remaining $0.073 of D700's ledger. Then the paid run is decided.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
